    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. that makes sense. so when i'm looking at the joist hangers and the connection points between the beams and the posts, what are the most common ways those connections fail or get compromised over time? just want to know what to look out for during inspections.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The connections are often the weakest links, and I've seen a few things that cause issues over time. A common failure point is **wood-to-wood movement** or improper fastening, especially with the joist hangers. If the hanger isn't perfectly seated or the nails/screws aren't driven deep enough, the connection can loosen under cyclical loading.\n\nAnother major issue, particularly in a damp climate like Vancouver, is **rot and moisture intrusion** around the connection points. If water pools near the post-to-beam connection, or if the hardware itself rusts (if not properly galvanized or stainless steel), the integrity of the connection degrades rapidly. Always check that the flashing and drainage around the posts are perfect, as moisture is the silent killer of wood connections.\n\nFor inspections, look for signs of movement—cracks in the wood adjacent to the connection, or visible signs of corrosion on any metal fasteners. A good connection should be tight, rigid, and dry.",
      "source": "target",
      "gen_token_ids": [
